decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-1 · `sealTurn` takes a list of effects and the receiver's nullifiers
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-2 · Value is a kernel discipline: balance AND floor conserved per denomination
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-3 · Three ledger receivers; the well's own id is the denomination
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-4 · A credit line is a floor transfer out of the well, under the issuer's authority
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-5 · Methods move value: a method's effects are `Body` calls, derived for the calling object
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-5 · CLOSED against KERNEL-28 and LANG-23: what was accepted, what amended, what each side owes
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-6 · The escrow: its account is `accountId self asset`, funded through a v0 mutate grant in the same tree; the payout's guard is the kernel's `once`, not the floor
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-7 · Accounts are named by reference in `Body`: `AcctRef = well asset | of owner asset`; no LANG builtin
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-8 · D5's three landings, and D5c's concrete shape: a Draft per receiver, an `At`
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-9 · LEDGER owns `Ledger/` and `world/escrow/`, and commits under `ledger:`
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-10 · The escrow package as built: Ledger/Escrow.lean, its law's theorems over all data, ledger-sim with three plants
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-11 · On LANG's digest day the escrow's ids are `digest`, and the deposit's once key is a creator-set `nonce`
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-12 · The escrow says what it does: one doc line, in the surface source and the Lean package alike
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-13 · The escrow's payout theorem is of what the receiver runs: `RunCalls` of every successful `runMeasured` of the escrow package
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-14 · The one-payout theorem is of the receiver's runs: a chain of committed runs, each reading the slots the last wrote, pays out at most once; two kernel-evaluated runs meet its premise
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-15 · The one-payout theorem over the history the World admits: KERNEL's next-run link turns an admitted history around one object into a `Runs`
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-17 · Unspent credit sits: no sweep, no decay, no reclaim to a commons
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-16 · LEDGER-6
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-18 · The deposit's authority is exactly its amount: the cap is read off the escrow, and a deposit then a release are an admitted history
decisions/ledger.md:## LEDGER-19 · A grant moves at most what it had left, over every audited history: `grant_moves_le_cap`, a `Std.chain_sum` instance over Worlds
decisions/root.md:## ROOT-HBOX · main lives on hbox
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-0 · the row: a renderer and a driver, and nothing imports it
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-1 · law 1: state is carried only by re-genesis from a checked export
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-2 · cross-World replay: reproduced against the real `host`, routed to KERNEL-4
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-3 · backup = the three files in order; restore = `host check --anchor`
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-4 · the test founder signs with RFC 8032's published vectors
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-5 · THE BAR is PERF's; OPS builds no BAR driver
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-6 · one prevent list from both exit tables, a start limit, and a generated operator table
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-7 · the broker's production TLS anchors are a build-pinned generated file
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-8 · the keybroker unit, rendered from HOSTING-16's exit tables, checks its anchors at every start
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-9 · HOST-33's moved check, planted through the real exe; the operator runs `check`
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-10 · the node's two users, SOCKDIR and the writer's runtime directory are rendered; the nd-hosted follower waits for CLIENT-3's socket group
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-11 · the hosted follower is a template unit per World, its socket flat in /run/nd-follow
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-12 · the grain launcher is a template unit per World, rendered from HOSTING-19's two exit tables
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-13 · the follower's driver is an always-restarting `nd sync` per World, not a timer
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-14 · the units under systemd: keybroker needs AF_NETLINK; grain-launch depends on its driver
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-15 · a follow Store is JOINED from a signed invitation, never a reproduced genesis; the hosted follower's join is a hand-run operator verb
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-16 · the live harness judges only this run's journal: a cursor, planted
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-18 · the owner's audit of the six OPS-path landings made without an OPS lane
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-19 · THE PIN: api.anthropic.com's leaf key is shipped in deploy/tls/pins
decisions/ops.md:## OPS-20 · the re-genesis owed for LANG-76's Bell: no deployed World holds the old Bell
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-1 · THE BAR over sockets: real processes, one clock per `nd`, judged only when quiet
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-2 · THE BAR runs on Linux, in standing, and gates counts, never a clock
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-3 · PROPOSED to ROOT: deployed-parameter counts from THE BAR's own Store
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-4 · C6: THE BAR seals a base at its midpoint; the reopen reads base + tail
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-5 · THE BAR's reopen at the true worst cut: a sized, standing-only bench row
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-6 · The worst cut, measured in three tail shapes: the costliest is pinned, not assumed
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-7 · bar.sh measures only binaries that are this tree's, by lake's and cargo's own reports
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-8 · The worst World is the reader's bound: a synthetic base at worldBytesMax, scaled into standing
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-9 · A fourth worst-cut shape: the deployed Bell queue's rows tail
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-10 · The worst World at the derived bound; the worst cut after the shared empty digest
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-11 · The worst cut after the one-pass commit
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-12 · The worst cut, one bench per shape: every judged run bounded under the deadline
decisions/perf.md:## PERF-13 · THE BAR re-runs the stale the kernel declines, and the standing run forces one
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-1 · No package dependencies: Lean core + Std only
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-2 · The def/proof split is structural: a `Proofs` path component
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-3 · Gates judge a private copy of the exact commit, both by the parent's gates and its own
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-4 · One axiom census instead of per-theorem `#assert_axioms`
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-5 · The size ratchet's mechanics
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-6 · Law 8's "No import hubs" is unmeasurable as stated
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-7 · Commits stay inside their layer: gates/OWNERS
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-8 · `scripts/commit` is the only sanctioned way to commit; a commit is refused only for reds it introduces
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-9 · The waste ratchet measures from the sims' own output: `WASTE <op> <metric> <ratio>`
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-9 · A commit is CURRENT main + its paths, and a path is taken only from a worktree whose base for it IS main's
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-10 · No separate gate for "a commit reverts another layer's CROSS-LAYER hunk in its own file"
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-11 · One landing clone
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-12 · The TCB table is gated: what the tree trusts without a proof has a row, pinned to what is actually used
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-13 · A codec vector is immutable under an unchanged tag
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-14 · The ungated toy run is deleted; the judge writes its own PID
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-15 · A runner that cannot read a failure is not a runner: every verdict reader proves it can read red first
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-16 · A wall-clock deadline withholds a verdict, never gives one: a timed-out run is UNREAD, re-run once, and the commit is not judged
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-17 · A standing-only verdict row: `standing:EXE` runs in standing and never in pre-push
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-18 · A CROSS-LAYER reason names the owner of each foreign path
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-19 · The landing queue: behind-main is a judged merge, readiness order with an age bound, verdicts carried only under a byte-identical input key
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20a · A run is selected by every binary it runs, not only its own: spawning rows, declared drives, and names in the static key
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20b · The landed copy: every path is merged from what the lander recorded, never taken from the working tree; a landing that would revert a recent commit is refused `stale path` unless named; a head lander is never charged for a tie
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20 · Traced read-sets select the runs and key the reuse; the judge's code is main's, the candidate supplies only its table
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20d · A compile's reads are C, compared only against a compile; an escaped name invalidates the trace; an implied directory is not an extra
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20e · A judge table is an input of every row it judges: a changed waste ceiling selects the row that measured it, standing-only included
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20f · A dead lander's scratch is reaped at a later lander's start, by liveness only
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20b′ · A prior commit's change is contained when the copy holds it, whatever its context
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-22 · The fast landing and the convergence: a landing is judged by what it reaches, the whole system every 3 h, a red names the commits it read
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20k · A failure of the tracer is not a verdict: UNREAD(tracer), re-run once, twice NOT JUDGED; a retry that passes is a counted flake
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20j · A skipped cargo test is never ok: INCOMPLETE in pre-push, RED in standing; `cargo-untraced:` rows run without the tracer
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20g · Rows run concurrently on free judge slots, each in its own scratch; GATES-20l · the convergence carries what the delta cannot reach, for at most a day
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-23 · A whole-tree census of disjunctive theorem conclusions
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20m · Namespace read-sets use bwrap's host bind sources
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20n · Complete the host-view evidence for real bwrap rows
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20o · A completed pre-bind namespace child has a host-view read-set
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20p · A moved added theorem keeps its statement
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20q · The charter's 1 MB file law is a source gate
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20s · The statement census reads the escape's shape, not its name; a standing plant proves it can fail
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20t · The stale-path guard's `.lean` half is a declaration diff from the census environment, and a landing's map of what it deletes and restates is checked
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20u · The certificate pins what `grant_moves_le_cap` says and rests on, one level, and the closure reader goes
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20v · A definition's edited body is named by one line; the fast judge runs the axiom and statement census over its reach; a declared change of meaning is the spec layer's
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20w · The statement census reads what a membership determines, pushes negations, reads Boolean equations and walks `∀`
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20y · The miscompiled literal is read from the compiled C, a vector per literal class proves which classes break; the TCB table rows the syscall layer and compares its file pins
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-20x · THE BAR's clock is judged in every run, the charter's 1000 records on every full judge; the forced contention is an exact row
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-25 · Every run has a TMPDIR of its own; a row run again alone is checked on what both of its runs wrote
decisions/gates.md:## GATES-27 · The compiled exe is held to the interpreter's account: kernel-sim's seeds run compiled and under `lean --run`, and must print one byte-identical trajectory

