import Kernel.Cell
import Kernel.Bounds
import Codec.Stream

namespace Nanodregg.Kernel

/-- A denomination in a refusal's diagnostic text (`Reject` derives `Repr`): its bytes in hex. Not a
wire form: the wire carries `Reject.name`. -/
instance : Repr ByteArray where
  reprPrec b _ := .text (b.foldl (fun s x =>
    let d := fun (n : UInt8) => if n < 10 then Char.ofNat (48 + n.toNat) else Char.ofNat (87 + n.toNat)
    (s.push (d (x / 16))).push (d (x % 16))) "0x")

/-- What a value cell holds. -/
structure Value where
  denom : ByteArray
  balance : Int
  floor : Nat

namespace Value

/-- The kernel's keys in a value cell; every other slot is RAM. Disjoint from the receive layer's
account layout (owner at 1). -/
def denomKey : Nat := 8
def balanceKey : Nat := 9
def floorKey : Nat := 10

/-- A balance's slot bytes: CODEC's `i64` (the caller keeps the value within `Int64`). -/
def balanceBytes (b : Int) : ByteArray := Nanodregg.Codec.Stream.i64.bytes (Int64.ofInt b)

def balanceOf (b : ByteArray) : Option Int :=
  match Nanodregg.Codec.Stream.i64.decode b with
  | .ok v => some v.toInt
  | .error _ => none

/-- A floor's slot bytes: CODEC's `u64`. -/
def floorBytes (f : Nat) : ByteArray := Nanodregg.Codec.Stream.u64.bytes (UInt64.ofNat f)

def floorOf (b : ByteArray) : Option Nat :=
  match Nanodregg.Codec.Stream.u64.decode b with
  | .ok v => some v.toNat
  | .error _ => none

/-- A balance an `i64` can hold. -/
/-
# Hosting.Resident.Bell — what the resident decides, as pure functions (HOSTING-27, HOSTING-31)

The resident serves ONE bell (`world/resident/Bell.ob`, LANG-58: a queue of rings) through its
member socket:
- It reads the bell's description once (`read OBJ ?`, PROTOCOL-44). Its `core` line, the package
  cell's bytes, must decode to the Bell (`bellCore`: `Lang.Bell.shapeOk`, HOSTING-45), or the
  resident ends `bellShape notBell` before any read or ask: the object the config names is the
  program the theorems speak of. From it come the index of the
  method labelled `answer`, whose argument must be `answerArgTy`, and the relation `pending`, whose
  key must be `(notBefore nat) (agent digest)` and whose value holds the captured `ask` plan. No index,
  bound or relation layout is written into the program; the read's K is `pending`'s declared limit.
- Each tick it makes ONE read, `read OBJ pending K` (PROTOCOL-51's range read): every unanswered
  ring, one `Lang.Print.data` line per row, read with `Lang.Read.readData` at the relation's row
  type (`Lang.rowTy`). The range read refuses past K and never truncates, so with K the declared
  limit the lines are ALL the pending rings (`Protocol.Proofs.Command.rangeLines_reads`), in
  ascending key order, which is ring order (`Lang.Proofs.KeyOrder.range_keys_ascend_read`).
- It answers the lowest pending ring: `invoke OBJ <answer> ARG`, ARG `Lang.Print.data` of
  `(record (notBefore N) (ringText X) (ringer #H) (text T) (to A))`, X the ring's text and A its
  optional AIM (HOSTING-53: `(none)`, or `(some (record (cand #C) (method M) (target #O) (via #G)))`) as
  the pending row holds them. The bell (LANG-59) upserts `rings[k]` to an `answered (some (ask T))`
  with immutable `captured (ask (record (text X) (to A)))` and retracts `pending[k]`; its `rings`
  guard refuses the upsert unless the ring is unanswered and the capture echoed is the ring's own
  (`rung: answered`, `rung: text`), so exactly-once is a check on the row as it is, and an aim cannot
  be dropped or altered by the resident. For an aimed ring the bell's `answer` also makes ONE nested
  call, the forward of T to the aim's target (`Lang.Proofs.Bell.answer_run`): answered is delivered,
  in the same Turn. The resident neither reads nor pins the target: the aim it echoes is judged by
  the guard, the forward by the kernel's authority on the Bell object's grant (`via`).

Everything it reads and writes is Lang's one reader and one printer. It holds no copy of either.

**Exactly one ask per ring** (each ask costs the meter). A ring is its key `(notBefore, ringer)`
and its text. An ask is SPENT once its frame has been sent in full. From then on the ring's answer
text is decided, whatever comes back: the reply's text, or a short text naming what came instead.
That text is kept in the resident's volume, with the ring, until the ring leaves `pending`, so
neither a failed invoke nor a restart asks again. A ring that arrives BELOW the kept one (a ringer
may backdate its notBefore by up to the kernel's window) waits until the kept ring is delivered: the
kept ring is answered first wherever it stands, and only then is the lowest asked about
(`Proofs.Resident.decide_*`, over `decideTick`).
-/
